>less compelling reasons.

Well, let's see.  There's no difference to the one being killed, who is
dead either way.  There may be a difference to the one doing the killing,
if he chooses to make one; and there may be a difference to uninvolved
bystanders, if they choose to make one.  There is no physiological
difference between a killed-for-survival cow and a
killed-to-make-Gucci-handbags cow... they're both dead.  The difference is
all in your head.

>You may choose, in your system of ethics,  to
>not give that distinction any merit,  but it is there nevertheless.

No, you're precisely wrong -- the distinction arises only in systems of
ethics, because it's the motivation, not the mechanism, that you (Andy)
consider in order to make the distinction.  BANG!  A bullet goes through
the head of a rattlesnake.  That's the physical event.  At the physical
level, there's no difference whether the rattlesnake was about to bite me
or I just wanted a nice belt.  The snake is dead either way.
