Date: Fri, 10 Sep 93 13:22:39 -0700
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Telecommuting (was Technological Infrastructure)

Picking up on comments made by Tim May:

I've worked at full-time jobs in which telecommuting for a substantial
portion of my working hours was a substantial advantage both for me and
for my employers.  Part-time telecommuting might provide an evolutionary
path (for awareness, technology, employment conventions) to lead toward
more widespread telecommuting.  Not everyone has done it, so some brief
comments may be in order.

In the early 1980s, in my first job past graduate school, I worked as
a consultant for a branch of the Department of Energy, from my home in
Davenport, California (15-20 Km up the coast from Santa Cruz).  Most of
my work involved reading, thought, and interviews, so I had to travel
variously, both to local libraries and to assorted distant cities, but
during portions of the data-reduction phase I was logged in to a mainframe
in Washington, DC, running jobs.  These days there would be other ways
to get the database access and cycles, but nonetheless, remote access
to a capability allowed me to do my job with that many fewer trips to
DC (expensive for my employer, unenjoyable for me).

During the last five years, I have occasionally worked in a mode in my
present job which had me spending much of the time at home, working
a great deal in the late afternoons and evenings, but on call at other
times.  I was writing microcode and assorted system software for a
new computer, and the way it worked was that my colleagues would find
things for me to do or fix, and I would attempt to fulfill their wishes
while they slept, or occasionally with on-call turnaround.  My personal
circumstances as a single "night person" allowed us to make better
use of scarce hardware (*the* prototype) by keeping it running at night.
(And as a startup, we were way too small for systematic multiple shifts.)
And it made more sense for me to be able to walk away from my terminal
and fix dinner or do chores during the lulls, than to sit idly in my
cubbyhole at work.

                                -- Jay Freeman

