In #93-9-366 James Donald writes:

If we were to ask them for the reasons for the violent and
lethal conflict, one or both would probably give an
explanation that would be equivalent to saying that the
other one violated his rights, this being the usual reason
for violent and lethal conflicts.

How can rights be violated, if they do not exist?
===
Just because they claim rights were violated does not mean that rights were
violated, or even exist.  In fact, each regards the presence of the other
as inconvenient to his own goals (or perhaps survival.)  If I were the
first person on the island, I could say that I had a right to the entire
island, being the first one there, and the newcomer was violating my
property right.  The other person could say that he has a right to
survival, and by attempting to hold the entire island I'm violating his
right to the resources necessary to survive.


In #93-9-366 James also writes:

And why, if not because of the universal and unchanging
nature of man, do we wish to beat the crap out of the
person who violates the contract?
===
What are you trying to say here?  Yes, we have evolved with a general
survival instinct, due to the immense advantage animals with that instinct
have over those who don't have that instinct.  So if someone breaks a
contract with us we made to help ourselves, or they threaten us and our
property, we (barring opposing memetic influence, q.v. Gandhi) get mad.
You can see this as a "natural" source but it doesn't mean that that is
"right"; it is the way things are.  I wish to defend myself, so I say that
defending myself is right and attacking me is wrong.  You say the same
thing about yourself.  We may agree that attacking each other is wrong, and
live in mutually assured peace, or I may attack you to obtain your
resources (maybe I'm a cannibal! <munch>)  You call that wrong; I'd call it
right.  How can your rightness be proven, short of beating me?

