Dave Krieger advises:

> third-person animal-morphisms are getting REALLY TIRESOME

I stumbled on the First Extropian Squirrel in a thought experiment and
elaborated the construct for reasons that I thought might be useful to
others as well as to myself, but will happily drop it if most people
wish me to do so (private EMail?).

My main reason for doing so was to encapsulate and clearly identify a
discussion position I was interested in, that involves a different
personal style from the one I might take in most face-to-face conversation,
and from the one I have taken in postings to previous Extropian threads.
Thus, the F. E. S. is more contentious and more certain of his beliefs
than I think myself to be in person.  Given his outlier position on a
controversial issue, such a style is perhaps more reasonable and/or more
fun than frequent repetitions of

    ... actually I'm not really as extreme as all that and I
    do agree with you in part, but as a thought experiment let's
    consider the following position blah blah blah ...

In any case, the intent was that readers who encounter the F. E. S.
(who speaks in third person so you won't confuse him with his
puppeteer), may anticipate one mindset, yet expect another when they
see posts from me which do not mention him.

Use of the squirrel also allows me to maintain a certain emotional
distance from a discussion that has occasionally shown some signs of
warmth -- after all, criticisms of the F. E. S.'s positions aren't
really criticisms of me, are they? -- at least I can allow myself to
think so.  I hope that such detachment has improved the quality of my
contributions, to the advantage of all.

Although the squirrel is contentious, I have tried to keep him at all times
both polite and respectful of conventions of scholarly debate.

If anyone who objects to the squirrel can also suggest ways to improve
the utility of such literary devices, please do so.

                                        -- Jay Freeman

