I know; I also know that the situation is very complex to
begin with (which was going to be the point of the essay-
that-will-not-be-written); but someone ought to look at why
California is losing jobs to Oregon and not to Louisiana,
what with Louisiana's lower wages and more easily bribable
officials in charge of enviornmental enforcement.

Those are the official H. Ross Perot reasons why there will
be an Enourmous Sucking Sound of US jobs heading south to
Mexico; but Louisiana has had free trade with California for
(let's see... 1872 sound like a good date?) 121 years, and
there hasn't really been an enourmous sucking sound.

The only industry we have that California doesn't is offshore
oil drilling. (Side note: has everyone heard about the projected
discovery in the Eugene Island Oil Block?)

California has been losing jobs and businesses to the intermountain
area and other Pacific states for a while for other reasons than
lower wages. 

It also lost a bunch of jobs to Georgia for the simple reason
that that's where Sam Nunn (D-GA), the head of the Senate
Armed Services Committee, is from.

For more info about that, read some background on the ATF
development program and read between the lines while you do.

   Major magazine pieces on this, including cover stories in the weekly
   mags about how the Golden State has lost its glitter. I think even
   "The Economist" has done an analysis of our woes.

I am aware of all of that; it ruins what would otherwise have
been a great Perot-bashing essay.

   (As usual, my caveat is that I am not endorsing these views or
   suggesting government needs to "do something" (except get lost, of
   course). I'm just letting Phil know that his predictions are several
   years too late.)

   > and why didn't Louisiana, with its much lower wage rate (although
   > I seem to be on anecdotal ground here), didn't benefit.

   Low wage rates are not everything. 

That was going to be the whole point of the "great sucking sound"
essay.

   Technological infrastructure and
   the overall investment climate plays a big role, as does the local
   work ethic. Relaxed New Orleans may be great for a vacation, but I
   can't imagine too many high tech startups choosing to start there.

Maybe, maybe not. The Acadiana area would be good if it had a better
educational infrastructure. (BTW: What's USL's national reputation
in computer science like?)

   But it may someday be a success. Resort areas, and New Orleans is
   certainly one of them, should do well in the telecommuting age. Of
   course, in the telecommuting age, only certain folks will be in
   demand--the clue-challenged masses will not be in much demand compared
   to their harder-working, better-educated counterparts in other regions
   of the world. I just don't see any scenario in which there are enough
   high-paying jobs for most people--but this is another debate.

   (I expect Duncan F. to challenge me on this! Perry M. I expect will
   agree with me, though. We'll see.)


But the whole point of a lot of these future changes will be 
to make education here better.

And don't statistics show that Americans are some of the hardest
workers in the industrialized world? Or most productive or something
like that?

(Why assume that the coming free-market revolution isn't going to
_help_ industries in the geographical area of the US greatly?)

