   Michael Morgan says:
   > I told a friend tonight that population pressures need not be
   > catastrophic, they just need to affect your quality of living.
   > Today my friend is hunting for a job in Atlanta. He's semi-freshly
   > graduated from Georgia Tech. and is in competition with thousands
   > of other recent-grads for a limited supply of jobs.
   > 
   > What do you propose as a solution for my friends 'real-time'
   > problem? Let's guard against gosh-wow futurism and get down to the
   > real nitty gritty.

   I suggest that he expands his job search beyond Atlanta. The bigger
   the pool of potential employers, the more likely he is to find someone
   who wants him.

   He could also lower his asking price, as it were.

   Perry

More to the point: how traceable are the current economic
problems to overpopulation? 

Our economy has growth problems right now; the US economy has
had much higher per capita and total growth of GNP during periods
of much higher population growth, and other countries with 
high pop. growth levels today have higher per capita and total
GNP growth figures than the U.S.


